+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+See proposal.md for motivation. Product reviews are represented by core review entities and exposed through shop DTOs, populators, facades, and controllers. Product stores a review average and count that currently require explicit lifecycle maintenance.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Enforce review ownership, uniqueness, and rating validation before persistence.
+- Use a dedicated public review representation that cannot serialize customer contact or address fields.
+- Centralize recomputation of product review average/count after every successful mutation.
+- Return stable HTTP status codes for validation, authorization, conflict, and missing-resource cases.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- Changing review moderation roles or authentication mechanisms.
+- Changing the product-review database schema unless the existing persistence API cannot support the required lookup.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Validate at the request/facade boundary and again at the service lookup boundary where needed. This prevents invalid writes and ensures rejected requests perform no mutation. A database-only constraint is insufficient because it cannot provide the required HTTP semantics.
+- Query reviews by product and customer before creating a review, returning a conflict when one exists. A unique database constraint may be added only if compatible with existing migrations, but the application check is required for the API response.
+- Compare the authenticated principal's customer id with the payload id before loading or creating the review. This prevents payload-based impersonation and avoids partial persistence.
+- Populate public review DTOs with explicit fields and first name only rather than exposing the customer entity graph. This is safer than relying on Jackson ignore annotations over a broad readable customer object.
+- Recompute aggregates from the authoritative reviews after create/update/delete, using `BigDecimal` division with scale 1 and `RoundingMode.HALF_UP`; write zero and zero when the review set is empty. Incremental arithmetic is avoided because edits and deletes are otherwise easy to drift.
+- Resolve merchant review targets by both review id and product id before mutation. This makes a cross-product id indistinguishable from a missing resource and guarantees HTTP 404 without changing another product.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Risk] Recomputing all reviews adds work for products with many reviews → keep the operation in the existing service transaction and use a repository aggregate query if the current API supports it; preserve the same rounding contract.
+- [Risk] Existing clients may expect customer fields in review payloads → the privacy requirement intentionally makes the public response narrower; update focused integration tests to lock the new contract.
+- [Risk] Legacy reviews may contain out-of-range ratings → validate all new and edited values; aggregate existing data as stored while tests cover the supported range.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+Deploy application changes with no required schema migration. Verify existing products with zero, one, and multiple reviews, then roll back the application version if needed; no destructive data migration is introduced.
